#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""./bench for a coding agent: an MCP server over the bench's socket.

    claude mcp add search-bench -- /path/to/Search/bench-mcp
    codex mcp add search-bench -- /path/to/Search/bench-mcp

It speaks MCP over stdio, one JSON-RPC message per line, and nothing but the
standard library. The agent calls open_session first, which starts this
checkout's build/Search.app hidden, in a world of its own (SEARCH_PROBE,
see fresh.sh): its own folder, settings and website data, nothing of the
browser someone is using in reach. Every other tool takes that session.
Nothing here reaches a Search this server didn't start: not the installed
one, with or without its switch, and not a test world someone else has
open. Every request first checks that the world's socket is held by the
process the session launched.

The contract is ArcMod's (github.com/CryogenicPlanet/arc, notes/computer-
use-tools.md), on top of the bench's own verbs:

- A handle names exactly one thing and fails rather than finds a
  substitute. Page handles (p1) belong to their session; element handles
  (p1.s3.e12) belong to one read_page, and the next read_page, or the page
  going somewhere else, ends them. A detached, hidden or covered element
  is an error, never a click somewhere near it.
- read_page gives roles, accessible names, values and boxes; a password
  or card field's value never leaves the page.
- Clicks are real mouse events (the bench's tap), typing is checked
  against what the field holds afterwards, and wait_for waits on a
  condition the agent names. A timeout never repeats an input.
- One controller per world: a lock held for the session's life, released
  by the system even when this process is killed.

A world this server named (agent-…) is wiped when its session closes, as
the tests wipe theirs (Tests/split_view.py); a world named by the caller is
started and kept, and refused if something already has it open.
"""

